<p><strong>Where to save money:</strong> Don't buy \"microgreen kits\" on Amazon. They charge $30 for a tray, some soil, and a packet of seeds. Buy the parts separately and you cut the cost by more than half.</p>

<p><strong>Where not to save:</strong> Buy organic seeds from a reputable supplier. Seeds sold for sprouting are tested for pathogens. Cheap garden seeds might be coated with fungicides you don't want on food you eat raw.</p>

<h2 id="steps">3. The 4 Step Growing Method (Day by Day)</h2>

<p>Here's the exact process I follow. It takes 10 minutes of hands on time. The rest is waiting.</p>

<h3>Day 1. Soak and Sow</h3>
<p>Fill your tray with 1 to 2 inches of coco coir or seed starting mix. Wet it until it's damp but not soggy. Sprinkle seeds densely across the surface. You want them close together but not stacked. About 1 tablespoon of small seeds (broccoli) or 2 tablespoons of large seeds (sunflower) per 1020 tray. Press them gently into the soil. Cover with the humidity dome. Put the tray somewhere dark and room temperature.</p>

<p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Large seeds like sunflower and pea need a 4 to 6 hour soak before planting. It speeds up germination by 1 to 2 days. Small seeds like broccoli and radish don't need soaking.</p>

<h3>Day 3 to 5. Uncover and Light</h3>
<p>By day 3, you'll see tiny white root hairs and the first shoots. Remove the humidity dome and move the tray under light. A <a href="/posts/best-led-grow-lights-for-beginners">basic LED grow light</a> works great. Keep it 4 to 6 inches above the tray and run it 14 to 16 hours a day. Water from the bottom by pouring water into the outer tray. Top watering knocks over the delicate shoots.</p>

<h3>Day 7 to 10. Watch Them Go Vertical</h3>
<p>This is where it gets fun. The shoots double in size every 24 hours. You'll see the first set of true leaves appear. Keep the soil moist but not wet. If you see mold or a sour smell, you're overwatering. Cut back on water and increase airflow.</p>

<h3>Day 10 to 14. Harvest</h3>
<p>Harvest when the first set of true leaves are fully open and the stems are 2 to 3 inches tall. Use sharp kitchen scissors and cut just above the soil line. Don't pull the plants out, you'll get soil in your harvest. Rinse gently and pat dry.</p>

<p>One tray gives you roughly 6 to 12 ounces of microgreens depending on the variety. That's the equivalent of 3 to 6 bags of store bought salad mix at $4 to $5 each.</p>

<h2 id="mistakes">4. 3 Mistakes That Killed My First 2 Trays</h2>

<p>I failed twice before I grew a successful tray. Here's exactly what went wrong so you can skip the expensive lessons.</p>

<p><strong>Mistake 1: Overwatering.</strong> I treated microgreens like houseplants and soaked them every day. The soil got waterlogged, the roots rotted, and the whole tray developed a white fuzzy mold. Fix: water from the bottom only, and only when the surface looks dry. Touch the soil. If it feels damp, leave it alone.</p>

<p><strong>Mistake 2: Not enough light.</strong> My first tray sat on a kitchen counter with indirect window light. The microgreens grew tall, pale, and floppy. They fell over like they had no backbone. That's called \"leggy\" growth. Fix: put them under a <a href="/posts/best-led-grow-lights-for-beginners">grow light</a> 4 to 6 inches above the tray for 14 to 16 hours. The difference is night and day.</p>

<p><strong>Mistake 3: Seeding too sparsely.</strong> I sprinkled seeds like I was planting a garden. Microgreens need to be packed tight. Sparse seeding means the little stems can't support each other and they fall over. Fix: seeds should cover the surface completely with almost no gaps. You want a \"carpet\" of greens.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">The Test I Use to Check Seeding Density</div>
  <p>After sowing, look at the tray from above. If you can see more than 10% of the soil surface through the seeds, you're not seeding densely enough. Add more seeds.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="blackout">5. The Flavor Hack Most People Don't Know About</h2>

<p>Here's a trick that separates restaurant quality microgreens from boring ones: the blackout period.</p>

<p>For 2 to 4 days before harvest, cover the tray with another empty tray or a dark cloth. Leave it in the dark. The microgreens think they're being buried and stretch upward looking for light. This makes them grow taller, with bigger seed leaves and thinner stems. The texture gets tender and the flavor gets sweeter.</p>

<p>Sunflower shoots especially benefit from this. Without a blackout, they taste like grass. With a 3 day blackout, they taste like sweet cucumber. It's the same plant with dramatically different flavor.</p>

<p>I use a second 1020 tray as a lid. Stack it directly on top. After 3 days, I uncover them, give them 1 day under light to green up, then harvest.</p>

<h2 id="vs">6. Microgreens vs Sprouts: What's the Difference?</h2>

<p>This is the most common question I get from friends. They're different things and people confuse them all the time.</p>

<p><strong>Sprouts</strong> are germinated seeds eaten whole including the seed, root, and shoot. They grow in water (no soil), take 3 to 7 days, and are eaten raw. The famous risk with sprouts is bacterial contamination because the warm, wet conditions are perfect for pathogens. Alfalfa sprouts and bean sprouts are the classic ones.</p>

<p><strong>Microgreens</strong> are grown in soil or a growing medium, harvested above the soil line (no seed or root), take 7 to 21 days, and need light. The soil based growing method reduces bacterial risk significantly. You also get 4 to 40x the nutrients compared to sprouts and mature vegetables.</p>

<p>If you're growing indoors for your kitchen, microgreens are safer, more nutritious, and taste better. Sprouts belong in the debate about whether they're worth the risk.</p>

<h2>Best Varieties for Beginners</h2>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Easiest. Grow These First</div>
  <p>• <strong>Radish:</strong> Ready in 5 to 7 days. Spicy kick. Nearly impossible to fail.<br>
  • <strong>Broccoli:</strong> Ready in 8 to 10 days. Mild flavor. 40x more nutrients than full broccoli.<br>
  • <strong>Sunflower:</strong> Ready in 10 to 14 days. Nutty, crunchy. Best texture of all microgreens.<br>
  • <strong>Pea shoots:</strong> Ready in 10 to 14 days. Sweet, tender. Regrows after cutting (unique).<br>
  <br>
  <strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=microgreen+seeds+starter+pack&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Get a starter seed pack here</a></strong> that includes all 4.</p>
</div>

<div class="callout" style="background: #fff4e5; border-color: #ffb74d;">
  <div class="callout-title">Skip These Until You Have 2 Successful Trays</div>
  <p>• <strong>Amaranth:</strong> Tiny seeds, slow germination, finicky about moisture.<br>
  • <strong>Beet:</strong> Slow (14 to 21 days). Needs consistent moisture.<br>
  • <strong>Cilantro:</strong> Very slow germination (5 to 7 days just to sprout).<br>
  <em>Start with the easy 4 above. Build confidence first.</em></p>
</div>

<h2>How Much Can You Actually Save?</h2>

<p>Let's put real numbers on this. I run 4 trays on a 2 shelf wire rack with one <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=LED+grow+light+for+microgreens&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">LED grow light bar</a> per shelf.</p>

<ul>
  <li>Material cost per tray: $3.50 (soil + seeds)</li>
  <li>Yield per tray: 6 to 12 ounces</li>
  <li>Store equivalent: $18 to $36 per tray (at $3/oz for organic microgreens)</li>
  <li>Savings per tray: $14.50 to $32.50</li>
  <li>4 trays every 2 weeks: $58 to $130 in savings per cycle</li>
  <li><strong>Yearly savings (26 cycles): $1,508 to $3,380</strong></li>
</ul>

<p>That math assumes you're replacing store bought microgreens. If you currently buy salad greens instead, the savings are smaller but the nutrition is much better.</p>

<p>I've been doing this for 5 months now. The setup cost paid for itself in the first week. Everything since then has been pure savings and better tasting food.</p>

<h2>Your First Move</h2>

<p>Order a 1020 tray, a bag of coco coir, and a packet of radish seeds. Total cost: about $13. While they ship, find a spot on your kitchen counter or a shelf that can hold a 10 by 20 inch tray.</p>

<p>Day 1: soak the coco coir, fill the tray, sow the seeds, put the dome on. Day 7: you're eating radish microgreens on everything. Sandwiches, salads, eggs, soups. Day 14: you're figuring out which variety to grow next and whether you should set up trays 2 and 3.</p>

<p>The hardest part is waiting the first 7 days. After that, you'll be harvesting greens faster than you can eat them. That's a problem I'm happy to have.</p>

